#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73ad4cd2-e7f0-5b7d-82fc-1a8e4b0597d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Hypotenuse of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Legs"}] :question/id #uuid "d1277863-7d33-566d-91b9-7a78458dca8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3077" :question/problem "If the side that is opposite to a $45^\\circ$ angle in an isosceles right triangle has a length of $3$, what is the length of the hypotenuse?\n\n![](images/11/11036dee7004fef1c43977a8a80bb46d4277834e558ac926b851d3fd5639965b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are the sides that are opposite the $45^\\circ$ angles. So in the given triangle, the legs have length $3$.\n\nConsequently, the hypotenuse has a length of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 3 = 3\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5faa905-dcfc-5eed-b4ed-5818ca6ef342" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Legs of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Hypotenuse"}] :question/id #uuid "ce112319-10a9-5483-82c3-566c0630b23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3080" :question/problem "If the hypotenuse of a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle has a length of $5$, then what is the length of each leg?" :question/worked-solution "The hypotenuse in a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $5$. So, if the length of a leg is $a$, then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot a \\\\\na &= \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\na &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ae8076e-25bc-5489-ae14-ffa16d17a32c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of the Diagonal or Side of a Square Given its Perimeter"}] :question/id #uuid "0d244991-c12e-5c56-8f58-762d8be22f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3082" :question/problem "Find the length of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ of the square $ABCD$ if the perimeter of the square is $p=256\\,\\mathrm{mm}$." :question/worked-solution "Since the perimeter is\n\n$$\np=256\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nthe length of a single side is\n\n$$\nAB = \\dfrac{p}{4} = \\dfrac{256}{4} = 64\\,\\mathrm{mm}\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$, which is an isosceles right triangle since\n\n$$\n\\angle{ABC} = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $AB=BC$.\n\n![](images/5b/5bb28a161b88919351ee8e83c2943817f20c124d80fb24c4ceffe29129fbbb07.png)\n\nThe hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, the length of a leg is $AB=64$, so the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot AB = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 64 = 64\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the square $ABCD$ has a length of\n\n$$\nAC= 64 \\sqrt{2} \\, \\textrm{mm}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16fd63e8-664f-53c2-b623-c696b86b5642" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Hypotenuse or Leg of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given its Perimeter"}] :question/id #uuid "e4466ef9-61d2-5b02-960b-c5c421a9dc1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3085" :question/problem "The perimeter of the triangle below is $26+13\\sqrt{2}$. Find the measure of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/d1/d194f2c2ce34bd40f5da5dd9e30048a6cc9318a1308c8a7e5d7156cb58a76134.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC = AB\\cdot\\sqrt{2} = a\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is\n\n$$\np = 26+13\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + AC \\\\\n26 + 13\\sqrt{2} &= a + a + a\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n26 + 13\\sqrt{2} &= 2a + a\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n13(2 + \\sqrt{2}) &= a(2 + \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n13(2 + \\sqrt{2}) &= a(2 + \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n13 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $a=13$, and the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = a \\sqrt{2} = 13 \\sqrt{2}\n$$"}]}
